then of course, there are the skill modifiers, V's can get basic in sabre
and expert in long sword, so
if single weaponing,
Excalibur gets +2 damage and +3 to hit
Grayswandir gets +0.   However, Grayswandir still beats out Excalibur on
anyhting but the max damage against large creatures (and even then, it's
just 33 vs 32)

if #twoweaponing
Excalibur gets -3 to hit and  +1 to damage
Grayswandir gets -7 to hit and -1 damage.  So they end up about the same as
they do for single weapon combat (i.e. Excalibur wins against max damage vs.
large creatures).  Note that the to-hit penalties/bonuses are fairly
meaningless, as you'll just about never miss in any case once your character
is in the later stages of the game (max luck, etc.)

Grayswandir does an insane amount of damage vs. demons as well, and you can
#twoweapon it with a silver saber and not have to practice both weapons
first.  Excalibur gives automatic searching, which might be useful but
really isn't necessary, it also confers level drain resistance, but that too
really isn't all the necessary.  Grayswandir gives hallucination resistance,
and is the only item in the game that can do so (not that hallucinations are
all that tough to deal with either)  Excalibur makes a great early game
weapon for a lawful character, but if given the choice between GS and EX,
use the GS.




